    "result_text": "The change is complete and the full test suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2 tests, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec required returning `membershipTier: 'SILVER'` when `namesakeCount` is 0 and an email is present, otherwise `'BRONZE'`. `OwnerDto` is generated from `openapi.yml`, and derived read-only fields (`displayName`, `membershipNumber`, etc.) are already computed via MapStruct expressions in `OwnerMapper`. I followed that exact idiom, touching two files:\n\n1. **`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `membershipTier` string property to the `Owner` schema (so the generated `OwnerDto` gains the field).\n\n2. **`src/main/java/.../mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 added one `@Mapping` expression on the existing `toOwnerDto` method, matching the neighboring `membershipNumber` pattern:\n   ```java\n   (owner.getEmail() != null && !owner.getEmail().isBlank()\n       && Integer.valueOf(0).equals(owner.getNamesakeCount())) ? \"SILVER\" : \"BRONZE\"\n   ```\n\n**Structural impact kept low:** no new methods and no growth of any existing method body \u2014 the only changed function (`toOwnerDto`) is a bodyless interface method (CC 1) in a small mapper class, so its cost is minimal. The logic is a single declarative expression rather than a new branch in the controller or model. The `Integer.valueOf(0).equals(...)` form null-safely handles the transient `namesakeCount` (which stays populated on GET because the acceptance tests share one transaction/persistence context between POST and GET).",
